1.1.text:
Article 8

1. The prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women is carried out in the following sectors: a. education;
b. health;
c. religion;
d. security;
e. justice;
f. public works and spatial planning;
g. public housing and residential areas; h. social affairs;

8 -
i. labor;
j. women's empowerment and child protection; k. defense;
l. environment;
m. population administration and civil registration; n. population control and family planning; o. transportation;
p. communication and informatics;
q. cooperatives, small and medium enterprises;
r. youth and sports;
s. culture;
t. tourism;
u. trade and industry; and v. other sectors.
2. The prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out by: a. developing communication media, information, education and public campaigns through media;
b. developing educational materials and curriculum according to regional authority;
c. developing safe transportation systems and public spaces;
d. building early detection systems, security and integrated complaint services in industrial areas, companies, residential environments, educational environments, boarding educational environments, and other public spaces;
e. implementing consultation services for families, prospective brides and grooms, students, Islamic boarding school students, youth, university students and vulnerable groups;
f. encouraging behavioral change of parents and society;
g. developing regional policies, schools, boarding schools, and universities that are safe and friendly for women and integrated with regional spatial planning policies;
h. forming and developing cadres, communities, young people, students, Islamic boarding school students, university students, journalists, social media influencers, educators, community leaders and religious leaders for prevention;
i. conducting education and advocacy to owners, managers and/or users of social media;
j. developing protection systems and special support for vulnerable women groups;
k. conducting awareness-raising for perpetrators;
l. increasing the capacity of the City Regional Government, government agencies, educational institutions, religious institutions, health service provider institutions, professional institutions, business sector, service institutions, youth organizations, legal aid organizations, media, religious leaders, community leaders and communities;
m. conducting cooperation with religious institutions, community social institutions, service institutions, - 9 - universities, media, youth organizations, religious leaders and community leaders; and n. conducting socialization of laws and regulations. (3) The sectors and forms of prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) are implemented by agencies, regional apparatus, institutions, community institutions, organizations, communities, professional institutions that handle related affairs according to their authority based on the provisions of laws and regulations.

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD,

MAYOR OF CIREBON,

Considering:
a. that every woman has the right to obtain a sense of security and protection from all forms of violence, exploitation, torture, discrimination, and other mistreatment that degrades human dignity and violates human rights;
b. that to ensure the prevention and handling of acts of violence against women quickly, accurately, comprehensively, and in an integrated manner, it is necessary to regulate the protection of women;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women from Violence, Exploitation and Discrimination;

In View of:
1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95);
3. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning the Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635), as amended by Law Number 31 of 2014 concerning the Amendment to Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning the Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 293, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5602);
4. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
5. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic - 2 - of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning the Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
6. Law Number 30 of 2014 concerning Government Administration (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 292, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5601), as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning the Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
7. Law Number 12 of 2022 concerning the Criminal Act of Sexual Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2022 Number 120, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6792);
8. Law Number 17 of 2023 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 105, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6887);
9. Law Number 108 of 2024 concerning Cirebon City in West Java Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2024 Number 294, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 7045);
10. Government Regulation Number 28 of 2024 concerning the Implementing Regulation of Law Number 17 of 2023 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2024 Number 135, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6952);
11. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 4 of 2023 concerning Acting Governor, Acting Regent, and Acting Mayor (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 310);
12. Decree of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 100.2.1.3-6572 of 2023 concerning the Appointment of Acting Mayor of Cirebon, West Java Province;
13. Regional Regulation of Cirebon City Number 9 of 2016 concerning the Establishment of Regional Legal Products (Regional Gazette of Cirebon City of 2016 Number 9), as amended by Regional Regulation of Cirebon City Number 5 of 2020 concerning the Amendment to Regional Regulation Number 9 of 2016 concerning the Establishment of Regional Legal Products (Regional Gazette of Cirebon City of 2020 Number 5);
14. Regional Regulation of Cirebon City Number 5 of 2021 concerning the Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus of Cirebon City (Regional Gazette of Cirebon City - 3 - of 2021 Number 5, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Cirebon City Number 107);
15. Regional Regulation of Cirebon City Number 3 of 2023 concerning Government Affairs Administered by the Regional Government of Cirebon City (Regional Gazette of Cirebon City of 2023 Number 3, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Cirebon City Number 128);

With Mutual Approval

Article 7

The prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women is intended to create a safe environment for women and to reduce the vulnerability to violence, exploitation and discrimination of vulnerable women groups, as follows:
a. women in disaster and social conflict situations; b. women with disabilities; c. women with HIV/AIDS;
d. migrant women workers;
e. girls;
f. women heads of households;
g. domestic women workers;
h. home-based women workers;
i. elderly women;
j. informal women workers;
k. women in situations of intolerance; and l. other vulnerable women groups.

CHAPTER IX

OBLIGATIONS OF THE CITY REGIONAL GOVERNMENT

Article 30

1. The obligations of the City Regional Government in the implementation of Women's Protection are as follows: a. formulate policies and include Women's Protection programs in regional development planning and budgeting;
b. provide complaint services, health, social rehabilitation, legal assistance and enforcement as well as repatriation and social reintegration for women victims;
c. establish shelter services and/or safe houses; d. establish a special mechanism for integrated services based on hospitals;
e. establish a PPT;
f. establish a UPT for Women and Children Protection; g. formulate standard operating procedures and service referral systems;

16 -
h. carry out prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women;
i. provide human resources and infrastructure for Women's Protection;
j. increase support and develop women's protection service institutions owned by the City Regional Government and the community;
k. develop community-based victim services, boarding educational institutions, basic educational institutions, religious institutions, and the business sector;
l. carry out capacity building;
m. carry out coordination and cooperation;
n. carry out empowerment to increase community participation;
o. develop an integrated and integrated women's protection data and information system; p. carry out control, guidance and supervision of the implementation of women's protection;and q. provide awards to the community, community organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, boarding educational institutions, the business sector and other parties.
2. Planning and budgeting for Women's Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a shall be the responsibility of Regional Apparatus implementing supporting functions of Government Affairs and the Agency.

CHAPTER II

WOMEN'S RIGHTS

Article 5

Every Woman has the right to:
a. obtain information related to the Protection of Women, victim service procedures and developments in case handling;
b. report, provide statements and views without pressure;
c. participate in case handling and in efforts - 7 - to eliminate violence against women;
d. obtain protection of confidentiality; e. obtain protection for the security of self, family and property from threats and violence;
f. be free from all forms of stereotypes, stigma and other discrimination;
g. obtain services and recovery needed; h. obtain restitution; and i. form and join organizations.

Article 3

Women's Protection aims to:
a. prevent all forms of violence, exploitation, and discrimination against women;
b. provide services and recovery for women victims of violence, exploitation and discrimination; c. realize the capacity of the City Regional Government in the implementation of Women's Protection from violence, exploitation and discrimination;
d. increase community participation in Women's Protection; ande. realize a social life that is safe and free from all forms of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women.

Article 4

The scope of regulation for the implementation of Women's Protection includes:
a. women's rights;
b. prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women;
c. services;
d. coordination and cooperation;
e. community participation;
f. data and information systems;
g. institutions;
h. obligations of the City Regional Government;
i. control, guidance and supervision; j. awards; andk. financing.

Article 12

1. Forms of protection services for women victims of violence, exploitation and discrimination, include services for: a. complaints;
b. health rehabilitation;
c. reproductive health;
d. social rehabilitation;
e. social reintegration; and f. legal aid and assistance.
2. Services for women victims are implemented quickly, comprehensively, with quality and integrated with services provided by the City Regional Government, society, educational institutions, boarding educational institutions, religious institutions, professional institutions, business sector through an integrated service system.

Article 19

1. Social reintegration services as referred to in Article 12 paragraph (1) letter e, are implemented with the following activities:a. repatriation and/or pick-up of victims; b. population document or identity services; c. alternative family;
d. scholarships and educational support facilities; e. non-formal education;
f. skills training and economic business;
g. financial assistance and business capital;
h. economic business assistance;
i. food and/or nutrition assistance for victims and their families;
j. provision of social protection programs and poverty alleviation programs;andk. other forms of repatriation and social reintegration services needed by victims.
2. Social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1), are provided to victims, families and people whose lives depend on victims with the aim that victims have the readiness and ability to live life in society.
3. Social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1), are carried out in an integrated manner with the Integrated Referral Service System for Social Protection and Poverty Alleviation and the Child Social Welfare Center.
4. Social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1), become the responsibility of the Regional Apparatus in charge of social affairs, population administration and civil registration affairs, labor affairs, small and medium cooperative business affairs, education affairs, health affairs, public works and spatial planning affairs, land affairs, women's empowerment and child protection affairs and other related affairs.

Article 11

1. Services are implemented to fulfill the rights of women victims and are carried out with the following principles: a. respect for human rights; b. non-discrimination;
c. confidentiality;
d. empathy;
e. inclusion;
f. empowerment;
g. best interests of the victim;
h. honesty;
i. professionalism;
j. integration; and k. sustainability.
2. Services to women victims must consider special conditions and needs based on the vulnerability experienced by women as referred to in Article 7.

